BitchX for Windows 11
=====================

Last Updated: 2024

This document describes how to build and run BitchX on Windows 10/11
using modern toolchains (Visual Studio 2022, CMake, vcpkg).


Prerequisites
-------------

1. Visual Studio 2022 (Community or higher)
   - Install the "Desktop development with C++" workload
   - Ensure the Windows 11 SDK (10.0.22621.0+) component is selected
   - Ensure CMake tools for Windows are included

2. vcpkg (for dependency management)
   - git clone https://github.com/microsoft/vcpkg.git
   - cd vcpkg && bootstrap-vcpkg.bat
   - Set environment variable: VCPKG_ROOT=C:\path\to\vcpkg

3. Git for Windows (for cloning the source)


Building with CMake (Recommended)
----------------------------------

Method 1: Command Line (Developer Command Prompt)

  Open "x64 Native Tools Command Prompt for VS 2022" and run:

    cd BitchX1.3
    cmake --preset x64-release
    cmake --build --preset x64-release

  The binaries will be in build/x64-release/

  For ARM64 Windows 11 devices:

    cmake --preset arm64-release
    cmake --build --preset arm64-release

Method 2: Visual Studio 2022 IDE

  1. Open Visual Studio 2022
  2. Choose "Open a local folder" and select the BitchX1.3 directory
  3. VS will detect CMakePresets.json and configure automatically
  4. Select the desired preset (x64-debug, x64-release, etc.)
  5. Build -> Build All

Method 3: VSCode with CMake Tools Extension

  1. Install the "CMake Tools" extension in VSCode
  2. Open the BitchX1.3 folder
  3. Select a configure preset from the status bar
  4. Click Build


Build Options
-------------

The following CMake options can be toggled:

  BITCHX_WITH_SSL       Enable SSL/TLS support (default: ON)
  BITCHX_WITH_GUI       Build Windows GUI version (default: ON)
  BITCHX_WITH_PLUGINS   Enable plugin/DLL support (default: ON)
  BITCHX_WITH_IDENTD    Enable built-in IdentD server (default: ON)
  BITCHX_WITH_SOUND     Enable sound support (default: OFF)

Example:
  cmake --preset x64-release -DBITCHX_WITH_SOUND=ON


Build Outputs
-------------

Two executables are produced:

  WinBitchX.exe  - Windows GUI version (recommended for Windows 11)
  BitchX.exe     - Console version (for Windows Terminal / PowerShell)


Windows 11 Features
--------------------

The modernized build takes advantage of these Windows 11 features:

- Per-Monitor V2 DPI Awareness
  Sharp rendering on mixed-DPI multi-monitor setups.
  The window automatically rescales when moved between monitors.

- Dark Mode Support
  Automatically detects the system dark/light theme preference.
  Title bar and colors adapt to the selected theme.
  Responds to real-time theme changes.

- Visual Styles (Common Controls 6.0)
  Modern control appearance matching the Windows 11 design language.

- Rounded Window Corners
  Uses the Windows 11 rounded corner style.

- UTF-8 Process Code Page
  Native UTF-8 text handling without explicit codepage conversions.

- Segment Heap
  Uses the modern Windows heap allocator for better performance.

- Long Path Support
  Supports paths longer than MAX_PATH (260 characters).

- Winsock 2.2
  Modern networking stack with full IPv6 support.

- Windows 11 on ARM
  Native ARM64 build support for Surface Pro X and similar devices.


SSL/TLS Support
---------------

SSL is managed through vcpkg. If VCPKG_ROOT is set correctly, OpenSSL
will be automatically downloaded and built as part of the CMake configure
step.

To connect to IRC servers with SSL:
  /server -ssl irc.libera.chat 6697


Directory Structure
-------------------

  win32/bitchx.manifest  - Application manifest (DPI, themes, compat)
  win32/bitchx.rc        - Windows resource file (version info)
  include/win32_compat.h - Windows compatibility layer header
  CMakeLists.txt         - CMake build system
  CMakePresets.json      - Build presets for VS2022
  vcpkg.json             - Dependency manifest


Troubleshooting
---------------

Q: Build fails with "cannot find openssl"
A: Ensure VCPKG_ROOT environment variable is set and vcpkg is bootstrapped.
   Run: vcpkg install openssl:x64-windows

Q: Window appears blurry on high-DPI displays
A: Ensure you're using the CMake build (not an older MinGW build).
   The manifest enables Per-Monitor V2 DPI awareness.

Q: Dark mode title bar doesn't work
A: This requires Windows 10 20H1 (build 19041) or later.
   Fully supported on Windows 11.

Q: ARM64 build fails
A: Install the ARM64 build tools in Visual Studio Installer
   (Individual Components -> MSVC ARM64 build tools).


Legacy Build Methods
--------------------

The original Cygwin/MinGW build path is still supported via the
autotools build system (./configure && make). See the top-level
INSTALL file for details. However, the CMake build is recommended
for Windows 11 as it provides proper DPI awareness, dark mode,
and modern Windows SDK integration.
